---
import { PLANS, formatKalVoiceAllowance } from "@kalcode/protocol/plans";
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { RELEASES, servedStableRelease } from "../../lib/releases";
import { KALVOICE } from "../../lib/site";

// KalVoice is announced as shipped only while /download serves a signed Stable release.
const stable = servedStableRelease(RELEASES);
// Push to talk as shipped (docs/KALVOICE.md; Fn on macOS since 0.1.7): Fn on a Mac, F8 fallback.
const FALLBACK_KEY = KALVOICE.pushToTalkKey;
---
<Docs
  path="/docs/kalvoice"
  heading="KalVoice"
  lead="The reference for KalVoice, the voice layer built into KalCode: the push-to-talk key, counting, providers and privacy."
>
  <p>
    {KALVOICE.summary}
    {
      stable
        ? `KalVoice ships in KalCode Stable ${stable.version}. Speech recognition and command interpretation run on your computer; KalCode downloads and verifies their signed local components by itself when you first open it, with no separate Python or Ollama installation.`
        : "KalVoice is in development and not available in a public build yet."
    }
    For the overview, see the <a href="/kalvoice">KalVoice page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>One key</h2>
  <p>
    Hold <kbd>Fn</kbd> on a Mac, or <kbd>{FALLBACK_KEY}</kbd> on any keyboard, to talk to KalVoice:
    hold it, speak, release. There is one gesture and no mode to pick. KalVoice decides from context
    what you meant.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="What happens when you release the key" tabindex="0">
    <table class="table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">What KalVoice does with what you said, and how it counts</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">You said</th>
          <th scope="col">Result</th>
          <th scope="col">Counting</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Words for the focused input</th>
          <td>Transcribed on your device and typed into that input (dictation).</td>
          <td>Never counted. Unlimited on every plan.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">A command, such as “Pause every active thread”</th>
          <td>KalCode does it and reports back.</td>
          <td>Local commands are never counted against cloud quota.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    On a Mac, hold Fn on its own and wait for Listening before you speak; a quick tap keeps Fn's
    usual action, and Fn with another key never starts dictation. {FALLBACK_KEY} is the fallback key
    on every keyboard and the one to use on Windows, where most keyboards don't report Fn to apps.
    In Settings you can choose another function key, Pause, Scroll Lock or Insert as the fallback
    instead, and KalCode checks it for conflicts with its own bindings. The key works while KalCode
    is the active window, so other apps keep it otherwise.
  </p>

  <h2>Dictation</h2>
  <p>
    Hold the key, speak, and release. The microphone starts the moment you press the key. When you let go, a speech-recognition model running on your own computer turns the audio
    into text, and KalVoice types it into whatever you were focused on:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>a Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or Gemini CLI agent's terminal,</li>
    <li>a thread composer or another terminal,</li>
    <li>the command palette, a search box, or another KalCode text input.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The target is fixed when you press the key, so switching windows mid-sentence cannot send
    your words somewhere else. Press <kbd>Esc</kbd> to cancel.
  </p>
  <p>
    In a thread's message box, a hint names the provider, thread and account KalVoice is writing
    to while you hold the key, and your words go only into that thread's box. In a terminal,
    KalVoice types your words but never presses Enter or runs a command.
  </p>
  <p>
    KalCode gets its default English speech model by itself the first time you open it, from
    KalCode's signed component catalog on kalcoded.com, and verifies its signature and SHA-256
    checksum before using it. The download waits while your computer is short on resources and
    while you are holding the key. If you remove the model, KalCode doesn't download it again on
    its own. Other speech models are downloaded only when you choose them.
  </p>

  <h2>Command mode</h2>
  <p>Hold the key and say what you want done, for example:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Go to the dashboard.”</li>
    <li>“Pause every active thread.”</li>
    <li>“What needs permission?”</li>
    <li>“What are my threads doing?”</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Structured commands like these are understood directly by KalCode and run without any AI model.
    More complex interpretation requires KalVoice's on-device runtime. When it is unavailable,
    KalVoice reports that limitation without sending your request to a connected provider.
    Unavailable requests do not consume your allowance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once the speech model is ready, KalCode prepares that on-device runtime by itself (about
    850 MB). You can pause it, and a download that fails is retried automatically. To download it
    only when you choose, for example on a metered connection, turn off
    <strong>Prepare local intelligence automatically</strong> in KalVoice settings.
  </p>

  <h2>Voice and your threads</h2>
  <p>KalVoice can put your words into a thread and send them, always through that thread's own Send:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      “Send that” sends what is in the focused thread's message box. “Clear that” removes only the
      text KalVoice typed there since the last send or clear, and never text you typed yourself; if
      KalVoice can't tell which text it typed, it changes nothing and says so. Neither sends
      anything else, and neither counts as a KalVoice Request.
    </li>
    <li>
      “Tell Authentication to run the tests” or “Ask Research why the build failed” opens that
      thread and sends the rest of what you said, in your words. You can name a thread by its
      provider and account too, such as “Research on Codex B”.
    </li>
    <li>
      When more than one thread fits, KalVoice asks, for example “Which one — Release Windows or
      Release Mac?”, and sends nothing until you click one or say its name.
    </li>
    <li>
      “What needs permission?”, “Open the one that failed” and “Go back” find and open threads by
      what they are doing or by where you just were.
    </li>
    <li>
      “It” means the thread in front of you, or else the one your last command was about, as in
      “Tell it to continue”.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Your prompt still goes through the thread's prompt review. KalVoice never sends to a thread
    that is waiting for your permission and never resumes a stopped thread to send to it.
  </p>

  <h2>KalVoice Requests</h2>
  <p>
    Each cloud-backed KalVoice request counts once, however many steps it takes.
    Local/on-device commands, dictation and voice into terminals do not consume cloud quota.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      PLANS.map((plan) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{plan.name}</strong>: {formatKalVoiceAllowance(plan.limits)} KalVoice Requests a
          month
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    Dictation and voice into terminals run on your device and are never counted, and neither is
    the usage on your provider accounts. When you reach
    your monthly allowance, dictation and the rest of KalCode keep working; cloud requests pause
    until the allowance resets or you change plans. See <a href="/pricing">pricing</a> for details.
  </p>

  <h2>Your provider, your account</h2>
  <p>
    Coding tasks you intentionally send to Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or Gemini CLI use your connected
    provider account and its native permissions. Provider usage is handled by that provider.
    KalVoice's local command interpretation does not use a paid provider fallback.
  </p>

  <h2>Private by default</h2>
  <p>
    Audio is captured into memory, transcribed on your device, and then discarded. Recordings are
    not stored or uploaded. KalCode's event log records that a dictation or request happened, never
    what you said or typed. Spoken replies, if you turn them on, use your operating system's own
    speech synthesis.
  </p>

  <h2>Inside your permissions</h2>
  <p>
    KalVoice calls the same runtime as the rest of KalCode: the same workspaces, terminals, agents
    and permissions. Ordinary workspace controls require no extra KalCode approval. Provider coding
    tasks keep their native permissions, and KalVoice cannot raise its own authority or answer a
    request for credentials on your behalf. Read the <a href="/docs/permissions">permission model</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
